      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE AND IMPORTANCE",
          "content": "We present a case report of a patient with a left frontal brain abscess. Cultures obtained from the abscess at the time of surgery were identified as dental flora known to establish a synergistic relationship in polymicrobial infections. This type of synergistic relationship makes the clearance of an infection more difficult for an intact immune system. A serum immunoglobulin (Ig) Type A deficiency was identified postoperatively. This immunodeficiency may have contributed to the development of the abscess."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CLINICAL PRESENTATION",
          "content": "The patient presented with headaches and photophobia. Computed tomography of the head performed with intravenously administered contrast demonstrated a left frontal brain abscess."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION",
          "content": "The patient was operated on through a left frontal approach, carefully avoiding the frontal sinus. The abscess was aspirated, and the patient was treated with intravenous antibiotics for several weeks. Postoperatively, the patient did well. There were no signs of enhancement on follow-up computed tomographic scans at 7 and 12 months postoperatively."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Through a comprehensive immunological workup, an IgA deficiency was identified postoperatively. Although the deficiency of a single type of Ig may be asymptomatic, complications from recurrent or chronic bacterial infections may occur. The deficiency of IgA, combined with a synergistic polymicrobial infection, contributed to the development of an intracranial abscess. A patient presenting with a brain abscess without any predisposing medical history should be evaluated for an underlying immune deficiency."
        }
      ]},
